WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.740
We can detect planets outside our solar
system by the gravitational effects

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.200
that these planets have on their stars.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.860
Small planets, like earth,
weigh much less than their star.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.520
When those planets move in their
orbits, the effect on the star

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.850
is very hard to see.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.640
The force they exert on each
other is comparatively small.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.040
So the star barely moves as
the planet orbits around it.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.090
Large planets, like Jupiter, have
a greater gravitational influence

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.145
on their star.

00:00:27.145 --> 00:00:31.680
But when the planets are far away,
the effect is still fairly small.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.260
The planet and star are
orbiting a common centerpoint,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.870
but it's close to the
center of the star.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.610
However, we found planets
that are larger than Jupiter,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.340
and which orbit much closer to their
star than Jupiter is to our sun.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.070
The effect of gravity is
much stronger in these cases.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.730
With such large planets, it's easier to
see that the planet's gravity affects

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.490
the star's motion, and that
the two orbit a common center.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.440
We can't see the star
move back and forth,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:05.590
because the stars are very, very,
very, very far away from us.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:08.050
Instead we use the Doppler
effect, the same effect

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.590
that causes sound to change
pitch when it's source moves.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.000
When a source of waves moves towards
us, we here it as higher pitched.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.310
When the wave source moves away
from us, we hear a lower pitch.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.440
In terms of light
frequency, that corresponds

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.410
to light moving more towards
blue, higher frequency.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.320
Or more towards red, lower frequency.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.220
The effect is small, so we don't
see it in our day to day lives.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.010
But we can detect it
with a spectrometer.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.250
As the exoplanet moves away from
us, the star comes towards us.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.960
So the light becomes ever
so slightly more blue.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.910
On the other side of the orbit,
as the star moves away from us,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.010
the light from the star
becomes slightly more red.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.020
The amount of shift, the
difference from blue to red light,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.900
tells us how fast the star is moving.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.830
That, in turn, tells us whether we
have a light planet, a medium planet,

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.270
or a heavy planet affecting
the movement of the star.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.539
And that's the wobble method.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.070
-It's supposed to be spinning.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.619
That's true.

00:02:11.619 --> 00:02:14.410
That's one thing we don't have
right in this, is it's not spinning.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.130
OK, when you're ready.

